#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fe5d8aa-8a79-5de4-8566-1432a640d78f" :knowledge-point/title "Equating Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Number to Solve For an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/id #uuid "fbe381a7-e6fb-52c1-bfdb-b77fd90a4b6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5245" :question/problem "Given that $3+2\\textrm{i} = a + \\textrm{i}b$, calculate the value of $a$ and the value of $b$." :question/worked-solution "First, we equate the real parts:\n$3 = a$\nThen, we equate the imaginary parts:\n$2 = b$\nTherefore, $a=3$ and $b=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bbe2fe7-b69c-54f3-9f1c-3369e3f1395d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Unknowns By Computing a Product and Equating Real and Imaginary Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "17e0ddb4-5a70-586b-9eec-e1cb584418e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5261" :question/problem "Given that $2 - 3 \\textrm{i} = \\dfrac{p - 2q \\textrm{i}}{1 + 2 \\textrm{i}}$, calculate $p$ and $q$." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $1+2\\textrm{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 - 3\\textrm{i} &= \\frac{p - 2q\\textrm{i}}{1 + 2\\textrm{i}} \\\\\n(2 - 3\\textrm{i})(1 + 2\\textrm{i}) &= p - 2q\\textrm{i} \\\\\n2 + 4\\textrm{i} - 3\\textrm{i} - 6\\textrm{i}^{2} &= p - 2q\\textrm{i} \\\\\n2 + \\textrm{i} - 6(-1) &= p - 2q\\textrm{i} \\\\\n8 + \\textrm{i} &= p - 2q\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n$8 = p$.\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n$1|=-2q; q|=-\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, $p=8$ and\n\n$$\nq = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9f40a0e-83f3-5814-b9a1-8b1265fe93cc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Unknowns By Computing a Quotient and Equating Real and Imaginary Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "746b590e-3b43-5de2-92fc-97ae7826c1fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5293" :question/problem "Given that $3+5\\textrm{i} = (p+\\textrm{i}q)(1+\\textrm{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q$." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $1+\\textrm{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + 5\\textrm{i} &= (p + \\textrm{i}q)(1 + \\textrm{i}) \\\\\n\\frac{3 + 5\\textrm{i}}{1 + \\textrm{i}} &= p + \\textrm{i}q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((3 + 5\\textrm{i})(1 - \\textrm{i}))/((1 + \\textrm{i})(1 - \\textrm{i})) &= p + \\textrm{i}q \\\\\n\\frac{3 - 3\\textrm{i} + 5\\textrm{i} - 5\\textrm{i}^{2}}{1^{2} - \\textrm{i}^{2}} &= p + \\textrm{i}q \\\\\n(3 + 2\\textrm{i} - 5(-1))/(1 - (-1)) &= p + \\textrm{i}q \\\\\n\\frac{8 + 2\\textrm{i}}{2} &= p + \\textrm{i}q \\\\\n4 + \\textrm{i} &= p + \\textrm{i}q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $4=p$.\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $1=q$.\n\nTherefore, $p=4$ and $q=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9f40a0e-83f3-5814-b9a1-8b1265fe93cc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Unknowns By Computing a Quotient and Equating Real and Imaginary Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4a3d761-7dba-5cc8-974c-f1d934ac16a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p = 1$, $q =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058982} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbfe6bb-6776-5984-bd78-49836b104618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p =-3$, $q =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058983} {:answer/id #uuid "1c77acd0-2324-555a-990a-d137c1c9d4fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p =-1$, $q =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058984} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0914e8-0b54-5949-bad9-74f229fa4af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p = \\frac{3}{2}$, $q =-\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058985} {:answer/id #uuid "592a59b3-8738-5092-b711-d565a43e8315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p = 2$, $q =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4a3d761-7dba-5cc8-974c-f1d934ac16a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p = 1$, $q =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058982} :answer-field/id #uuid "b375e472-6115-5807-9575-7cb1605d670f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058981}] :question/id #uuid "7075d093-3130-53bd-98c6-59bdcaa6e2bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-8036" :question/problem "Given that $5 - \\textrm{i} = (p + \\textrm{i}q)(3 + 2\\textrm{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9f40a0e-83f3-5814-b9a1-8b1265fe93cc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Unknowns By Computing a Quotient and Equating Real and Imaginary Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e4d90cf-c2bc-5f17-9de4-d5bcdd4ec332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p = 3$, $q =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059090} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b515b8-4de8-5a51-a183-5086358022db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p =-3$, $q = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059091} {:answer/id #uuid "b13c82fb-20c7-550d-a6de-81f7ec8f0c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p =-5$, $q = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059092} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2ff224-c650-5311-83ae-86e5a00ae889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p =-1$, $q = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059093} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7b2775-326b-5329-8f66-71639d76ca88" :an